  cites Gold chapter BlitGold

 Grade Safari chapter Blit -- the shading recipe and the visibility thresholds --
 against `web/blitter.js`.

 THE SECOND CHECK WHOSE ORACLE IS NOT A GAME MODULE. NumCheck grades Codex's Real
 primitives against zig's builtins because zig is what the game calls. This one
 grades against JAVASCRIPT, because JavaScript is where these particular decisions
 have always lived: a shading model and four thresholds, in the one file this
 project cannot run.

 THERE IS NO f32 GAP TO ABSORB, so everything here is graded exactly. Colours are
 integers and the predicates are booleans; the only Reals are inputs. That makes
 this a stricter check than most of judge/, and it should be -- there is nothing
 to be approximate about.

 THE INPUTS COME FROM THE GOLD, as in NumCheck: the generator prints its tables as
 `g-blit-*-in` lines and this chapter maps over them, so an input exists once
 rather than twice in two languages.

 THE LAST THREE GRADES ARE THE EXPANSION ITSELF, not its arithmetic, and they are
 what makes moving the recipe across the wire a checked change rather than an
 argued one. `blit-expand` and the generator's `expandShade` are two independent
 statements of the same rewrite -- which command becomes which tag, what it carries
 and what is dropped -- so comparing them is the same differential the rest of this
 repository runs between Codex and zig.

 We say:

Section: Fan Out

 The shade table is a colour list and a factor list of the same length, walked
 together -- the pairing the generator wrote them in.

  shade-pairs : List Integer, List Real, Integer -> List Integer
  shade-pairs (cs) (fs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [shade-color (list-at cs i) (list-at fs i)] & shade-pairs cs fs (i + 1)

 Each shading case contributes THREE colours -- edge, middle, edge -- so the answer
 is three times as long as the input, flattened in stop order.

  shade-triples : List Integer, List Real, Integer -> List Integer
  shade-triples (cs) (ss) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else (width-shade-stops (list-at cs i) (list-at ss i)) & shade-triples cs ss (i + 1)

  disc-pairs : List Real, List Real, Integer -> List Boolean
  disc-pairs (rs) (as) (i) =
    if i >= list-length rs then []
    else [disc-visible (list-at rs i) (list-at as i)] & disc-pairs rs as (i + 1)

  flat-pairs : List Real, List Real, Integer -> List Boolean
  flat-pairs (los) (his) (i) =
    if i >= list-length los then []
    else [too-narrow-to-shade (list-at los i) (list-at his i)] & flat-pairs los his (i + 1)

Section: The Expansion

 One case is a tag-1 command over a three-point polygon: the colour and the
 strength the gold names, the x's it names, and y's that are no part of the answer.

  exp-case : Integer, Real, Real, Real, Real -> DrawCmd
  exp-case (color) (strength) (x0) (x1) (x2) =
    let cmd = DrawCmd { tag = 1, color = color, color2 = 0, strength = strength, geom = [], pts = [x0, 10.0, x1, 20.0, x2, 80.0] }
    in expand-shade cmd

 Each case contributes its tag and its two colours, flattened.

  exp-ints : List Integer, List Real, List Real, List Real, List Real, Integer -> List Integer
  exp-ints (cs) (ss) (xs0) (xs1) (xs2) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else let d = exp-case (list-at cs i) (list-at ss i) (list-at xs0 i) (list-at xs1 i) (list-at xs2 i)
      in [d.tag, d.color, d.color2] & exp-ints cs ss xs0 xs1 xs2 (i + 1)

 And its span, which a flat fill does not have; the generator writes zeros there
 and so does this, rather than either side inventing a shape for a missing answer.

  exp-span : List Integer, List Real, List Real, List Real, List Real, Integer -> List Real
  exp-span (cs) (ss) (xs0) (xs1) (xs2) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else let d = exp-case (list-at cs i) (list-at ss i) (list-at xs0 i) (list-at xs1 i) (list-at xs2 i)
      in (if list-length d.geom == 2 then [list-at d.geom 0, list-at d.geom 1] else [0.0, 0.0]) & exp-span cs ss xs0 xs1 xs2 (i + 1)

Section: The Dropping

 A frame of nothing but droppable commands -- every disc case the gold holds, then
 every radial case -- run through the whole expansion. What survives, and in what
 order, is the answer: the thresholds are graded above, and this grades that
 `blit-expand` consults them and leaves everything else alone.

  mix-discs : List Real, List Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  mix-discs (rs) (alphas) (i) =
    if i >= list-length rs then []
    else let d = DrawCmd { tag = 3, color = 16711680, color2 = 0, strength = list-at alphas i, geom = [40.0, 50.0, list-at rs i], pts = [] }
      in [d] & mix-discs rs alphas (i + 1)

  mix-radials : List Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  mix-radials (rs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length rs then []
    else let d = DrawCmd { tag = 4, color = 1, color2 = 2, strength = 0.0, geom = [50.0, 50.0, list-at rs i], pts = [10.0, 10.0, 90.0, 20.0, 50.0, 80.0] }
      in [d] & mix-radials rs (i + 1)

  tags-of : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  tags-of (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).tag] & tags-of cs (i + 1)

  mix-tags : List Integer
  mix-tags =
    let frame = mix-discs g-blit-disc-r g-blit-disc-a 0 & mix-radials g-blit-radial-r 0
    in tags-of (blit-expand frame 0) 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "blit-shade " (shade-pairs g-blit-shade-c g-blit-shade-f 0) g-blit-shade)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "blit-crown " (shade-triples g-blit-crown-c g-blit-crown-s 0) g-blit-crown)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "blit-disc  " (disc-pairs g-blit-disc-r g-blit-disc-a 0) g-blit-disc)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "blit-radial" (list-map radial-visible g-blit-radial-r) g-blit-radial)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "blit-flat  " (flat-pairs g-blit-width-lo g-blit-width-hi 0) g-blit-flat)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "blit-expand" (exp-ints g-blit-exp-c g-blit-exp-s g-blit-exp-x0 g-blit-exp-x1 g-blit-exp-x2 0) g-blit-exp)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "blit-span  " (exp-span g-blit-exp-c g-blit-exp-s g-blit-exp-x0 g-blit-exp-x1 g-blit-exp-x2 0) g-blit-exp-span 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "blit-drop  " mix-tags g-blit-mix)
  end

